## Short answer  

**Yes – you can reuse the same `font-faces` block in a MapLibre GL JS style, but you have to serve the font files over HTTP (or HTTPS) so the browser can fetch them.**  

GL JS reads the `font-faces` property exactly like the native SDK (since v6.7.0).  
If the URLs point at files that are reachable from the web page, the browser will load those files as web fonts, apply the `unicode-range` you declared, and render the text locally without ever contacting a glyph-PBF server.  

The only things that differ from the native case are:

| Native | GL JS |
|--------|-------|
| Can read a file-system path (`file:///…`) because the app runs on the device. | Must fetch the font through the browser’s networking stack (CORS-allowed URL). |
| Offline works automatically once the app is installed. | You need to **cache** the font files yourself (e.g. Service Worker, `Cache` API, or `preload`/`prefetch` tags) if you want true offline use. |
| `font-faces` is read once at style load. | You can also call `map.setFontFaces()` at runtime to add or replace entries. |

Below is a complete checklist and a minimal example that shows how to get the same behaviour in a web app.

---

## 1. Make sure you are on a recent GL JS version  

* `font-faces` support was added in **v6.7.0**.  
* The local-font fallback (when `glyphs` is missing or a glyph PBF fails) works from **v5.11.0** onward.  

If you are using an older version, upgrade:

```bash
npm install maplibre-gl@^6.15.0   # latest stable at time of writing
```

---

## 2. Keep the `font-faces` block exactly as you have it in Native  

The JSON syntax is identical. Example (taken from your native stylesheet):

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": "https://example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "font-faces": {
    "MyBrand Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF"]          // Latin
      },
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular-Ext.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0100-024F"]          // Latin-Extended
      }
    ],
    "MyBrand Bold": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF"]
      }
    ]
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["MyBrand Regular", "Noto Sans Regular"],
        "text-size": 14
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**What you must verify**

| Item | What to check |
|------|---------------|
| **URL accessibility** | Open each `url` in a browser tab. It must return the raw `.ttf`/`.otf` file with a `200` response and **CORS headers** (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain). |
| **Correct font-stack names** | The strings used in `text-font` (`"MyBrand Regular"` etc.) must match the keys in `font-faces` **exactly** (case-sensitive). |
| **Unicode-range syntax** | GL JS expects an array of strings like `"U+0000-00FF"`; a single string is also accepted. |
| **Optional `glyphs` field** | You can keep it (it will be used for any code-points not covered by a `unicode-range`). If you want *pure* offline rendering, you can **omit** the `glyphs` property entirely – GL JS will then fall back to the local-font engine for *all* scripts. |

---

## 3. Serve the fonts in a way that works offline  

### 3.1. Simple caching with a Service Worker (recommended)

```js
// sw.js
self.addEventListener('install', (e) => {
  e.waitUntil(
    caches.open('maplibre-fonts').then(cache => {
      return cache.addAll([
        'https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf',
        'https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular-Ext.ttf',
        'https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.ttf'
      ]);
    })
  );
});

self.addEventListener('fetch', (e) => {
  const url = new URL(e.request.url);
  if (url.origin === 'https://example.com' && url.pathname.startsWith('/fonts/')) {
    e.respondWith(caches.match(e.request).then(resp => resp || fetch(e.request)));
    return;
  }
});
```

Register it in your page:

```js
if ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {
  navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js');
}
```

Now the fonts are cached the first time the page loads and will be available even when the network is gone.

### 3.2. Pre-load with `<link rel="preload">` (optional)

```html
<link rel="preload" href="https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf"
      as="font" type="font/ttf" crossorigin>
<link rel="preload" href="https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.ttf"
      as="font" type="font/ttf" crossorigin>
```

This speeds up the first paint but does **not** replace the Service Worker cache for true offline use.

---

## 4. Using `map.setFontFaces()` (dynamic scenarios)

If you need to add fonts after the map has already been created (e.g. user-selected theme), you can call:

```js
map.setFontFaces({
  "MyBrand Regular": [
    {
      url: "https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf",
      "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF"]
    },
    {
      url: "https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular-Ext.ttf",
      "unicode-range": ["U+0100-024F"]
    }
  ],
  "MyBrand Bold": [
    {
      url: "https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.ttf",
      "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF"]
    }
  ]
});
```

Calling this **replaces** the whole `font-faces` map, so you must provide *all* entries you still want to keep.

---

## 5. What about CJK, Arabic, Devanagari, etc.?

| Script | How GL JS draws it when `font-faces` is present |
|--------|-------------------------------------------------|
| **CJK (Han ideographs)** | By default GL JS uses the **local-ideograph** engine (`localIdeographFontFamily`). `text-font` is ignored for those code-points. If you *do* want to serve your own CJK glyph PBFs, set `localIdeographFontFamily: false` in the map options, but this is usually slower and unnecessary. |
| **Arabic / Hebrew (RTL)** | Since **v6.9.0** GL JS shapes RTL text itself; no `setRTLTextPlugin` needed. The browser’s font-fallback (or the `font-faces` entry you provide) determines the visual shape. |
| **Devanagari, Thai, Khmer, Myanmar, etc.** | From **v6.15.0** GL JS draws these scripts *as grapheme clusters* using the browser’s text engine, **ignoring the glyph-PBFs**. The `font-faces` entry you supply is the only way to control which font is used. |
| **Latin / Cyrillic / Greek** | Rendered from the SDF glyph PBFs **unless** you omit `glyphs` or the glyph request fails, in which case the local-font fallback (TinySDF) takes over. With `font-faces` present, the browser-font will be used for any code-point not covered by a glyph range. |

**Bottom line:** For any script that needs complex shaping (Arabic, Devanagari, etc.) you *must* provide a `font-faces` entry that points at a web-font file covering the appropriate Unicode range. The glyph server is ignored for those scripts in recent GL JS versions.

---

## 6. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – offline font demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>

  <!-- Pre-load fonts (optional, speeds first paint) -->
  <link rel="preload" href="https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf"
        as="font" type="font/ttf" crossorigin>
  <link rel="preload" href="https://example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.ttf"
        as="font" type="font/ttf" crossorigin>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
if ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {
  navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js');
}

// 1️⃣ Load the style (could be a local JSON file or an inline object)
fetch('style-with-font-faces.json')
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(style => {
    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: style,
      center: [0, 0],
      zoom: 2,
      // optional: keep CJK local (default) or change it
      // localIdeographFontFamily: '"Noto Sans CJK SC", sans-serif'
    });

    // If you need to add fonts later:
    // map.setFontFaces({ ... });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

`style-with-font-faces.json` is the same JSON you used in the native app (including the `font-faces` block). The only difference is that the URLs must be reachable from the browser.

---

## 7. Common pitfalls & troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Text appears in a generic system font** (e.g. Arial) | Font file not fetched (CORS error, 404, or blocked by CSP). | Open the Network tab → look for the `.ttf` request. Add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) on the server, or serve the file from the same origin. |
| **Labels disappear completely** | `glyphs` URL is present **and** the requested range returns 404, and the browser cannot find a matching `font-faces` entry for those code-points. | Either add the missing `font-faces` entry, or omit the `glyphs` field so GL JS falls back to the local-font engine. |
| **CJK characters look blocky** | `localIdeographFontFamily` is set to `false` (or you served CJK glyph PBFs that are missing many ranges). | Remove the option or set it back to `'sans-serif'` (or a better on-device CJK font). |
| **Arabic text is rendered left-to-right** | Using GL JS < 6.9.0 without the RTL plugin. | Upgrade GL JS or load `setRTLTextPlugin` before creating the map. |
| **Offline mode still tries to fetch fonts** | Service Worker not caching the font URLs, or you are using a different origin for the fonts. | Verify the Service Worker `install` event caches the exact URLs, and that the fetch handler returns the cached response. |
| **`font-faces` is ignored** | The style’s `glyphs` field is missing **and** you are on GL JS < 5.11.0. | Upgrade to ≥ 5.11.0, or keep a `glyphs` URL (it can point at a dummy server) – the presence of `font-faces` alone is enough once the version is recent enough. |

---

## 8. TL;DR checklist for copying the native `font-faces` to GL JS

1. **Upgrade** to MapLibre GL JS ≥ 6.7.0.  
2. **Copy** the `font-faces`

## Arabic / Hebrew labels on a MapLibre GL JS 6 map  
*(right-to-left layout + proper joining of Arabic letters)*  

| What you need | Why it matters | How to do it |
|---------------|----------------|--------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 6.9.0** | Since v6.9.0 the library ships its own HarfBuzz-based shaping engine. No external RTL plugin is required and the engine automatically handles right-to-left direction, Arabic contextual forms, and Hebrew mirroring. | Verify the version you load (e.g. `https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.9.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js`). If you are on an older 6.x release, upgrade or add the legacy RTL plugin (see the “Legacy fallback” box below). |
| **A font that contains the script** | The shaping engine picks a font by the `text-font` name you give the layer. If the font does not contain the needed glyphs the browser will fall back to a system font, which may look wrong or be missing some characters. | • Use a Noto family that ships the script you need (e.g. **Noto Sans Arabic**, **Noto Sans Hebrew**). <br>• Add the font to the style’s `font-faces` property (or load it as a web-font). |
| **`font-faces` entry (or a working `glyphs` URL)** | For Arabic/Hebrew the glyph-PBF route is *not* used for shaping – the engine draws the text with the font file you provide. The `font-faces` block tells both GL JS and Native where to fetch the TTF/OTF. | ```json\n\"font-faces\": {\n  \"Noto Sans Arabic Regular\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf\",\n      \"unicode-range\": [\"U+0600-06FF\", \"U+0750-077F\", \"U+08A0-08FF\", \"U+FB50-FDFF\", \"U+FE70-FEFF\", \"U+1EE00-1EEFF\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"Noto Sans Hebrew Regular\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansHebrew-Regular.ttf\",\n      \"unicode-range\": [\"U+0590-05FF\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```<br>*If you already serve glyph PBFs for those fonts you can keep the `glyphs` field, but the `font-faces` entry is still required for proper shaping.* |
| **`text-font` list on the label layers** | The list is interpreted as a CSS-like fallback stack. The first entry that the engine can find (via `font-faces` or a loaded web-font) is used for the whole label. | ```json\n\"layout\": {\n  \"text-field\": \"{name_ar}\",\n  \"text-font\": [\"Noto Sans Arabic Regular\", \"Noto Sans Regular\"],\n  \"text-direction\": \"auto\"   // optional – lets the engine decide LTR/RTL per label\n}\n```<br>Do the same for Hebrew (`\"Noto Sans Hebrew Regular\"`). |
| **`text-direction` (optional but recommended)** | `auto` makes the engine detect the direction of each label string and set the correct base direction. If you always know the language you can also set `"rtl"` explicitly. | Add `"text-direction": "auto"` (or `"rtl"`) to the symbol layer’s layout. |
| **CORS / font loading** | `font-faces` URLs are fetched by the browser, so they must be CORS-accessible (or served from the same origin). | Serve the TTF/OTF with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) or host them on the same domain as the page. |
| **Testing** | Verify that a label such as “الرياض” (Riyadh) appears with the correct joined forms and that the whole word is right-aligned. | Open the map, inspect a label with the browser dev-tools → “Computed → direction”. It should be `rtl`. |

---

### Legacy fallback (if you cannot upgrade to ≥ 6.9.0)

```javascript
import { setRTLTextPlugin } from 'maplibre-gl';

// Load the Mapbox RTL plugin *once*, before creating the map.
setRTLTextPlugin(
  'https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-rtl-text@0.3.0/dist/mapbox-gl-rtl-text.js',
  false   // false = do not wait for the plugin to load before creating the map
);
```

*After the plugin is installed the same `text-font` / `font-faces` setup works, but the plugin adds extra network requests and is slower than the built-in shaping introduced in v6.9.0.*

---

## Minimal working example (GL JS 6.12)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Arabic / Hebrew RTL labels</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.12.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.12.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        osm: {type: 'raster', tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'], tileSize: 256}
      },
      layers: [
        {id: 'basemap', type: 'raster', source: 'osm'}
      ],
      // ---- FONT SETTINGS -------------------------------------------------
      "font-faces": {
        "Noto Sans Arabic Regular": [
          {
            "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
            "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF","U+0750-077F","U+08A0-08FF","U+FB50-FDFF","U+FE70-FEFF","U+1EE00-1EEFF"]
          }
        ],
        "Noto Sans Hebrew Regular": [
          {
            "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansHebrew-Regular.ttf",
            "unicode-range": ["U+0590-05FF"]
          }
        ]
      },
      // optional glyphs URL (can be omitted if you only use local fonts)
      "glyphs": "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf"
    },
    center: [35, 31], // somewhere in the Middle East
    zoom: 5
  });

  // Add a simple symbol layer with Arabic and Hebrew names
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('places', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: {
        type: 'FeatureCollection',
        features: [
          {
            type: 'Feature',
            geometry: {type: 'Point', coordinates: [46.7, 24.7]},
            properties: {name_ar: 'الرياض', name_he: 'ירושלים'}
          }
        ]
      }
    });

    // Arabic label
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'arabic-label',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'places',
      layout: {
        'text-field': '{name_ar}',
        'text-font': ['Noto Sans Arabic Regular', 'Noto Sans Regular'],
        'text-direction': 'auto',
        'text-size': 14,
        'text-anchor': 'top'
      },
      paint: {'text-color': '#000'}
    });

    // Hebrew label
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hebrew-label',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'places',
      layout: {
        'text-field': '{name_he}',
        'text-font': ['Noto Sans Hebrew Regular', 'Noto Sans Regular'],
        'text-direction': 'auto',
        'text-size': 14,
        'text-anchor': 'bottom'
      },
      paint: {'text-color': '#000'}
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Key points in the example*  

* `font-faces` points at the TTF files (replace `example.com` with your own host).  
* `text-direction: "auto"` lets the engine decide RTL for Arabic/Hebrew.  
* `text-font` includes the script-specific font first, then a generic fallback.  

---

## Checklist for a production map

1. **Version** – `>= 6.9.0` (prefer the latest 6.x).  
2. **Fonts** – Host TTF/OTF for every script you use (Arabic, Hebrew, etc.).  
3. **`font-faces`** – Add a mapping for each `text-font` name, with an appropriate `unicode-range`.  
4. **Layer layout** – `text-font` list, `text-direction: "auto"` (or `"rtl"`).  
5. **CORS** – Ensure the font URLs are reachable from the browser.  
6. **Testing** – Verify direction, joining, and that the same font is used for the whole word (no mixed-font artifacts).  

Once those items are in place, MapLibre GL JS will render Arabic and Hebrew place names correctly: right-to-left, with contextual letter forms, and without needing the now-deprecated `setRTLTextPlugin`.

Below is a step-by-step recipe for adding a **hillshade** layer that draws its data from a **raster-DEM** source in a MapLibre GL JS map.  
All the code can be dropped into a normal MapLibre initialization script; no extra plugins are required.

---

## 1. Prepare the DEM tiles

The source must be a raster-DEM tile set (e.g. Mapbox Terrain-RGB, Open-Topography, or your own Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFF tiles served as XYZ/WMTS).  
The tiles have to be in **Mapbox Terrain-RGB** format (24-bit RGB where R,G,B encode elevation) or any other format that MapLibre can decode as a DEM (e.g. `image/png` with elevation encoded in the pixel values).

> **Tip:** If you already have a regular raster source (satellite imagery, etc.) you cannot use it for hillshade – you need a DEM.

---

## 2. Add the raster-DEM source to the style

```js
// Assuming you already have a `map` instance:
map.addSource('my-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  // URL template for the DEM tiles (XYZ, WMTS, or TileJSON)
  // Example using OpenTopoMap's DEM tiles:
  //   https://{s}.tiles.opentopomap.org/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png
  // Replace with your own endpoint.
  url: 'https://example.com/tiles/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // Optional: set the maximum zoom level the source provides.
  maxzoom: 14,
  // Optional: if the DEM tiles are not in the default 256 px tile size,
  // specify the size here (e.g. 512).
  tileSize: 256,
  // Optional: if the DEM uses a different encoding (e.g. 16-bit PNG),
  // you can tell MapLibre how to interpret it.
  // See the style spec for `raster-dem` for more options.
});
```

**Key points**

| Property | Why it matters |
|----------|----------------|
| `type: 'raster-dem'` | Tells MapLibre to treat the raster as elevation data. |
| `url` | Must point to a tile service that returns DEM tiles. |
| `tileSize` | Must match the size of the tiles you serve (default 256). |
| `maxzoom` | Helps the style engine know when to stop requesting higher-resolution tiles. |

---

## 3. Add the hillshade layer

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'my-dem',          // the source we just added
  // The source-layer is **not** needed for raster-dem sources.
  // If you have multiple DEM layers in a single source (e.g. a TileJSON with layers),
  // you can specify `source-layer: 'dem'` here.

  // Optional paint properties – tweak to taste
  paint: {
    // Light direction (azimuth) in degrees clockwise from north.
    // 315° = north-west (default)
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,

    // Light intensity (0-1). 0 = completely dark, 1 = full illumination.
    'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'viewport', // or 'map' for fixed lighting
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,               // exaggerate terrain relief
    'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)', // darken shadows
    'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)' // brighten highlights
  }
});
```

### Common paint properties for `hillshade`

| Property | Default | Typical range | What it does |
|----------|---------|---------------|--------------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | `315` | `0-360` | Sun azimuth. |
| `hillshade-illumination-anchor` | `'viewport'` | `'viewport'` | or `'map'` | Whether the sun moves with the map or stays fixed. |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | `0.5` | `0-2` | Multiplies the elevation to make relief more pronounced. |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` | `rgba(0,0,0,0.5)` | any CSS color | Color of shadows. |
| `hillshade-highlight-color` | `rgba(255,255,255,0.5)` | any CSS color | Color of illuminated slopes. |
| `hillshade-accent-color` | `rgba(255,255,255,0.5)` | any CSS color | Optional extra accent shading. |

---

## 4. (Optional) Add a terrain layer for 3-D extrusion

If you also want the map surface to be displaced in 3-D (e.g. for a 3-D view with `pitch` and `bearing`), enable the terrain source:

```js
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'my-dem',
  // Optional: exaggerate the terrain vertically.
  exaggeration: 1.5
});
```

> **Note:** The hillshade layer works **independently** of the terrain layer. You can have hillshade only, terrain only, or both together.

---

## 5. Full minimal example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Hillshade from raster-DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base map (e.g. OSM raster) – optional, just to see context
        'osm': {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'osm', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 60,
    bearing: -20
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add the DEM source
    map.addSource('my-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://example.com/tiles/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 14
    });

    // 2️⃣ Add the hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'my-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.7,
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.4)'
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ (Optional) Enable 3-D terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.2 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## 6. Troubleshooting checklist

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No hillshade appears** (map stays flat) | • DEM source URL is wrong or CORS-blocked.<br>• Tiles are not in Terrain-RGB format. | Verify the tile URL in the browser, check network tab for 200 responses, and ensure the image encodes elevation (e.g. open a tile in an image viewer and see the RGB gradient). |
| **Hillshade looks “pixelated” or missing at high zoom** | • `maxzoom` on the source is lower than the map zoom.<br>• Tile size mismatch (`tileSize` wrong). | Raise `maxzoom` or serve higher-resolution tiles; set `tileSize` to the correct value (256 or 512). |
| **Shadows are too dark / highlights too faint** | Paint values are at defaults. | Adjust `hillshade-shadow-color`, `hillshade-highlight-color`, and `hillshade-exaggeration`. |
| **CJK or other script labels disappear after adding hillshade** | Not a hillshade issue – usually a missing `glyphs` or `font-faces` configuration. | Ensure your style still has a valid `glyphs` URL or uses local fonts (outside the scope of hillshade). |
| **Terrain 3-D looks flat even after `setTerrain`** | • DEM source not added before `setTerrain`.<br>• `exaggeration` set to 0. | Call `setTerrain` after the source is added and use a non-zero exaggeration. |

---

## 7. Quick reference summary

| Step | Code snippet |
|------|--------------|
| **Add DEM source** | `map.addSource('my-dem', {type:'raster-dem', url:'…/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', tileSize:256});` |
| **Add hillshade layer** | `map.addLayer({id:'hillshade', type:'hillshade', source:'my-dem', paint:{…}});` |
| **(Optional) Enable terrain** | `map.setTerrain({source:'my-dem', exaggeration:1.5});` |
| **Common paint tweaks** | `hillshade-illumination-direction`, `hillshade-exaggeration`, `hillshade-shadow-color`, `hillshade-highlight-color`. |

That’s all you need to get a hillshade layer rendering from a raster-DEM source in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!
